Implement TASK-260922-31qyyi (derivation-side-profile-source-gate), the ONLY leaf of STORY-260922-cpkajd (lease-aware-profile-source-authority). Your Change Request is therefore **story_final** and carries the traceability registry work.

WHY THIS EXISTS — A HALF-CLOSED SECURITY PROPERTY. Pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` §2.4 (Execution profiles, line 646; the clause at line 666): losing-lease or ambiguous events MUST NOT change the effective profile. BUG-260917-3lddu0 (landed in PR #57, trunk `40bb8c9`) closed this only **through append admission**: `sessrepo.checkWinningLease` now refuses an append under a superseded lease. But `sessprofile.Derive` takes `(record, events)` and cannot see the lease store at all, so a losing event that extends its own lease's tail is a perfectly continuous chain to it. The 3lddu0 reviewer proved it: with the append gate disabled as an instrument in a copy, **probe 15 still yields `{Profile: yolo, HasSource: true}`** at `Projector.Project` — a losing writer drives the session to `yolo` with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`. Any other path that admits such an event (a future writer, a replay, a restore) reopens the hole. This leaf gives the property an owner **independent of the append gate**. Read BUG-260917-3lddu0's verdicts rev1-rev3 and its results before writing anything; the probe bodies are in `TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-evidence-rev1.tar.gz` (probes 9 and 15).

THE INVARIANT, to prove by construction: a `profile.changed` authored under a non-winning or ambiguous lease is **never the effective profile source**, at every derivation entry — `LoadProfile`, `Projector.Project`, `SetProfile`'s from-end, and `axpane.deriveProfile` — whether or not the append gate admitted it. Decide what the derivation needs to see (lease-store facts, provenance) and get it from the landed owners (`internal/sessrepo`, `internal/sessstate`, `internal/fencing`); do not re-model leases, epochs or digests locally.

**THE TEST TRAP THAT SANK THE LAST ATTEMPT.** In 3lddu0 rev1, `TestLosingLeaseProfileEventIgnored` asserted the append refusal FIRST and never reached its own derivation assertions — its kill was the append gate's kill, so one test "covered" both properties and the registry bound a clause to an owner that did not implement it. So: every witness for this leaf must drive the **derivation** with the append gate out of the way (a fixture that writes the losing event directly into the chain, or the gate disabled in the test's own copy), and must fail when the derivation-side check is removed. Ship a narrowing mutant on the derivation check; verify its kill by running the killer ALONE.

DECIDE TWO CLASSES THE PREVIOUS LEAF LEFT OPEN. (a) A **never-minted higher-epoch** lease: a `profile.changed` under an epoch above the winner is admitted by `checkWinningLease` today, on trunk and candidate alike, and becomes the profile source. A successor lease is always CAS-installed before its first event, so "authored under the winning lease exactly" would refuse the class without touching a legitimate flow — decide it, and §2.4's word "ambiguous" is your authority. (b) The **empty lease store** (no lease record: the gate is skipped). Pin whichever behaviour you choose with named tests.

STORY-FINAL REGISTRY WORK. Edit `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` — the ADOPTED registry. Bind the §2.4 clause to the owner that actually implements the property after your change, and make sure the acceptance case appears **in the clause's acceptance-case list**: 3lddu0 rev2 defined a case that appeared in ZERO clause lists, and tracecheck did not notice because it pins declarations, not edges — decode the registry and prove the edge exists. Re-derive `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` from the registry as it stands in your candidate. Run `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` and the section-scoped runs on the exact tree and quote the ratios verbatim; keep the README "Measured coverage" subsection equal to what tracecheck prints (the landed `readme_coverage_pin_test.go` pins it). LOGBOOK newest-first. Another Story (STORY-260830-2t4g7i) will also land registry rows; if trunk moves before your handoff, refresh and **MERGE** the registry, never replace it, and re-derive the digest after the merge.

CENSUS AND COMPOSITION. A gate × entry census as its own table in the conformance matrix and mirrored in TRACEABILITY: every derivation entry above, each cell a named test plus an admitting narrowing row, `unreachable` with a reason, or a stated bound with an owner. Composing writers must keep working: compare **runtime outcomes** keyed on `(package, entry, input)` over the complete importer set of `internal/sessprofile` on the base and the candidate, and name every moved class. Never rewrite a landed test to accept new behaviour without a spec citation.

WORKSPACE. This is a fresh single-leaf Story: the spawn provisions `.temp/STORY-260922-cpkajd/worktree` on `task-board/story/STORY-260922-cpkajd` from trunk `c9233ce`. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED tree there. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board`, never self-accept. Never run `git add`, `git add -N`, `git rm --cached` or `git reset` in the worktree except as the handoff requires; compare bytes through a scratch `GIT_INDEX_FILE`.

PROCESS. Read the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill first. All CI local; never trigger hosted CI. Run your package tests, the importer set, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` (command 5 is `-race`, 25 min; do not shorten; another Story is producing concurrently). Resolve every artifact path ABSOLUTELY and verify each attachment by reading it back off the board. Attach TASK-260922-31qyyi_results.md, TASK-260922-31qyyi_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260922-31qyyi_producer-evidence.tar.gz, keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260922-31qyyi --role developer` and exit. If the invariant needs a change in a landed owner that is not yours to make, STOP and name the owner and the contract rather than adding a local guard. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: gpt-6-luna max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5-5 low.
REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.
(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.
(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.
(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.
(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.
(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.
(f) Prose that outruns the tests. "Composes X", "never admits what decoding refuses", "nothing re-implemented", "no cached sentinel can authorize" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.
Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.
